ndards so the degree to which the  standards are really broad or narrow  affected how much competition came about  and whether they regulated all the  actors in the sector or a narrower  subset so a concrete example is fair  aid we yeah for coffee it's set up  initially in the Netherlands in the late  1980s and decided to focus only on  cooperatives so it only certifies fair  trade cooperatives and that meant that a  large portion of the global production  of coffee that comes from plantations  couldn't apply to the program because  they're not fair yeah so that was one of  the impetuses for another program to  enter the market to service that the  plantation coffee and then the final one  that I explores the role of market  demand and how market demand at that  early stage created more incentives to  enter or less incentives and internally  affected the interests of actors within  the programs to adapt or not to adapt  sir so why certify it's a great question  so that is one of the key challenges  these programs face and that's partly  why a lot of people are interested than  is that in the case of government when  government set rules governments can  sanction people if they don't adhere to  them so you can get fined if you don't  pay your taxes or if you really are bad  actor you can get thrown in jail and so  you can't do that with certification so  the main mechanism for sanctioning  essentially is demand in the marketplace  so consumerist you mean the end users or  do you mean something else yeah so  consumers play some role in some of the  programs so for instance I would imagine  some people would like with fair trade  coffee for instance some people would I  only drink fair trade coffee nothing  else in fact something like that yeah so  there's some of the labels that are much  more well known by end consumers so  organics are more well-known fair trade  it has a quite a large consumer yea high  levels of consumer awareness and but  even when people are awa